I have a javascript object that represents a processing video. As the video is processing, I send a request to the server every 5000 milliseconds, and once it is processed, it should unbind itself, and complete. The trouble is that every time it checks the server to see if it's complete, it re-renders an instance of itself, and binds an additional 5000 millisecond timeout. Consequentially an exponential loop occurs, and will eventually crash the browser.

I have a loop error occurring because of this event handler :

initialize: function() {
  this.on('all', this.reset_auto_refresh, this);
  this.auto_refresh();
},


Once this initialize() method is cast, it binds reset_auto_refresh and runs auto_refresh . 

auto_refresh: function(timeout) {
  if(this.is_processing()) {
    if(!timeout) timeout = 5000;
    var callback = _.bind(function() {
      this._timer = null;
      this.fetch({error: _.bind(function() { this.reset_auto_refresh(15000); }, this)});
      this.auto_refresh();
    }, this);
    this._timer = setTimeout(callback, timeout);
  }
},
reset_auto_refresh: function(timeout) {
  if(this._timer) clearTimeout(this._timer);
  this.auto_refresh(timeout);
}


Because every time it instantiates, it's technically a different object, I havn't figured out a way for any instance to share a single ._timer, and therefore, they keep adding on new setTimeout's even though one already exists and is running.

Would anyone know a best practice, or a way to prevent multiple instances setting a setTimeout here?

